<article aria-labelledby="adversarial-examples-for-fast-hash-functions" class="post layout-grid" id="article" tabindex="-1">
<div class="story-lead post-section l-body" id="overview">
<p class="l-body"><strong>A hash turns a message into a small fingerprint.</strong> Hash tables use that fingerprint to find an entry quickly. Two different messages with the same fingerprint are a <em>collision</em>; too many collisions can slow the table down. A secret random seed changes the fingerprints, making it harder for someone to prepare troublesome inputs in advance. But how much protection does it actually provide?</p>
<p class="l-body">For <a href="#lanes">komihash v5.27/v5.34</a>, I found two fixed 64-byte messages that differ in just two bits and collide for about <strong>91% of sampled random seeds</strong>. The attacker does not need to know the seed. In another example, the tested <a href="#appendix-a5hash-128">128-bit a5hash variant</a> passes all 250 published SMHasher3 tests, yet a fixed pair collides for <em>every</em> seed. A large output and a passing test suite are not enough to rule this out.</p>
<p class="l-body">The experiment is simple: choose two different messages first, then choose the secret key independently and see whether their hashes agree. Testing can expose a bad pair. A mathematical proof can go further and limit the collision probability for <em>every</em> pair; hashes with this property are called <a href="#proofs">universal hash families</a>. I compare both kinds of evidence below, alongside the speed of the implementations.</p>
<p class="l-body">In the chart, rightward is faster. The vertical <a href="#metric">collision score</a> expresses collision bounds in bits, adjusted for message length. A solid teal dot is a proved minimum; a rust mark is a maximum exposed by a particular colliding pair. A high rust mark does not establish safety—a worse pair may exist. Hollow dots show claims whose proofs remain unresolved. Select any hash for its background, result and assumptions.</p>
</div>
