<div aria-label="What to take away" class="story-takeaways post-section l-body">
<p class="l-body">The recurring mistake is losing information before the secret can help. If two messages have already become the same intermediate value, later mixing treats them identically. Other hashes split work into parallel calculations but leave exploitable relationships between them: in the <a href="#lanes">komihash example</a>, two carefully chosen changes largely cancel. Adding a seed is useful only if the construction uses it in a way that protects against these patterns.</p>
<p class="l-body">A proof also has assumptions that an implementation must meet. <a href="https://github.com/thomasahle/chainhash">ChainHash is my construction</a>; its 61-bit guarantee requires 80 independently random key bytes. The <a href="#appendix-chainhash128">128-bit version</a> gives a 125-bit guarantee with 160 random bytes. Generating a larger-looking key from one 64-bit seed does not supply that randomness. The profiles distinguish these mathematical guarantees from the key setup used to measure speed.</p>
<p class="l-body">A guarantee need not mean slow hashing: CLHASH and ChainHash have competitive throughput on large inputs in these benchmarks. <a href="#speed">Small inputs tell a different story</a>. On M2, hashing a 1–31-byte message takes ChainHash 70.49 cycles, compared with 21.08 for rapidhash v3 and 32.86 for PolymurHash. The main chart measures long messages; it should not decide which hash is fastest for a table of short strings.</p>
<p class="l-body">For inputs chosen without feedback about the hidden key, <a href="#choice">I would choose a hash with a proved bound</a> that covers how my program encodes inputs, generates keys and maps hashes to table slots. An attacker who can learn from earlier outputs or timing is a harder problem. That calls for a cryptographic keyed hash such as SipHash and an analysis of that feedback. The lesson is to ask what a hash guarantees for the way you use it, alongside how fast it runs.</p>
</div>
