<section aria-labelledby="metric" class="post-section l-page"><h2 class="l-body" id="metric">The collision score</h2><p class="l-body">The score puts collision probabilities on a manageable scale and adjusts for message length. For a given length, one extra bit in a <em>proved guarantee</em> halves the allowed collision probability. It is not the number of bits returned by the hash, and it does not say how much work an attacker must do. The formulas below make that distinction precise.</p><p class="l-body">A <em>hash family</em> is the collection of functions obtained by changing the secret key. To state a guarantee, we must specify the algorithm, allowed messages, output size and how the key is chosen. Call that key distribution <span class="math" data-tex="\mu">μ</span>. The family is <span class="math" data-tex="\varepsilon">ε</span>-<em>almost universal</em> if any two distinct messages, chosen before the key, collide with probability at most <span class="math" data-tex="\varepsilon">ε</span>. The probability is over the random key, not over randomly chosen messages.</p><p class="l-body">Measure the longer message in eight-byte units, rounding up, and call the result <span class="math" data-tex="L">L</span>: <span class="math" data-tex="L = \left\lceil \frac{\max(\operatorname{len}(m),\operatorname{len}(m\prime))}{8} \right\rceil">L = ceil(max(len(m), len(m′))/8)</span>, with a minimum of 1. For each allowed L, <span class="math" data-tex="\varepsilon^*(L)">ε*(L)</span> is the worst full-output collision probability among distinct pairs with exactly that rounded maximum length. Rounding is only for the score: it does not add bytes to the actual input. The byte-string experiments allow different message lengths; the fixed-length theorem rows compare equal lengths only.</p><p class="l-body">A proved score of s guarantees <span class="math" data-tex="\Pr[\text{collision}]\leq\min(1,L\cdot 2^{-s})">Pr[collision] ≤ min(1, L · 2⁻ˢ)</span> throughout the stated message domain. Thus a bound proportional to message length gets a constant score. For example, a 61-bit guarantee at L = 8 limits the collision probability to 8/2<sup>61</sup> = 2<sup>−58</sup>. This is a probability bound for a pair chosen before the key, not a claim of 2<sup>61</sup> work to attack the hash.</p>
<p class="l-body" id="pinned-length-comparison">Table 2 also compares the bounds at one common message size: 2<sup>30</sup> bytes, labeled “1 GB” in the table. That answers a different question: how small is the allowed collision probability for two files of that size? The column shows −log₂ of the probability bound, without dividing by length. UMASH-64’s entry uses the corrected certified bound; UMASH-128’s entry distinguishes its published claim from the inherited linear envelope. A collision found for two short messages tells us nothing about messages of that size, so I do not extrapolate the witness caps.</p>
<p class="l-body">HalftimeHash’s tree height grows with the logarithm of the length, and its stated core bound is quadratic in that height. This comes at the price of key material proportional to the supported height: fresh NH keys per level, reused across that level’s nodes. The shipped wrappers use 8,866 key words and support a fixed domain of about 2.7 GB (decimal) times the style width. Single-key polynomial constructions keep a constant key and pay linearly in length. For HalftimeHash I use complete groups of 144·b bytes, with b = 1, 2, 4, 8 for Style64/128/256/512, N = floor(bytes/(144b)), and h = floor(log₈(7N+1)) − 1. The <a href="#appendix-halftimehash">appendix</a> adds the wrapper’s independent tabulation term to the core bound.</p>
<p class="l-body">There is a tradeoff between how much random key material is available and how a bound grows with message length. With a constant-size key, the Singleton bound on the corresponding code gives ε ≥ (L − 1)/(number of keys). A t-word key therefore cannot give a uniform bound better than this scale of L/2<sup>64t</sup>. Using a larger field can make that growth negligible over practical lengths; using a tree can improve the length dependence over a supported range by allocating more key material as the permitted tree height grows. The exact constructions, rather than these rough scales, determine the bounds in Table 2.</p>
<p class="l-body">I retain the exact field sizes, block factors and output terms in Table 2. ChainHash (paper)’s (bytes/256 + 2)/2<sup>64</sup> comparison uses the <a href="#appendix-chainhash">41-independent-word reference</a>; the displayed model A has a different bound. The table below shows both. HalftimeHash’s core values appear below its full wrapper values. At 256 B, the wider styles have no complete group, so the stated height formula gives h = −1.</p>
