<p class="l-body"><b>Which ChainHash is plotted?</b> ChainHash is my construction. The M2 chart uses the <a href="#appendix-chainhash">paper’s 256-byte layout</a>; the Xeon chart uses <a href="records/chainhash/v2/SPEC.md">v2’s 1,024-byte layout</a>, which processes neighboring pieces together. They produce different hash values. Both have a 61-bit mathematical guarantee under their specified 80-byte random key setup. <a href="records/chainhash-integrate/STATUS.md">Lean checks the original construction</a>; adapting that formal proof to v2 is unfinished. The paper version’s 200/200 test result is not transferred to v2. ChainHash-128 is a separate 128-bit construction with its own key size and proof.</p><p class="l-body">Following the paper and audit, I first clip a bound at 1 and floor it at <span class="math" data-tex="2^{-r}">2<sup>−r</sup></span> for an r-bit output. This scoring convention prevents an injective short-input case from receiving infinite credit. It does not give a lower bound on a particular pair’s collision probability.</p><div class="math-display l-body" data-tex="\text{family score} = \inf_{\text{admitted }L}\log_2\!\left(\frac{L}{\max\!\left(2^{-r},\varepsilon^*(L)\right)}\right)" data-tex-compact="\begin{aligned}&amp;\text{family score}\\[0.35em]&amp;\quad = \inf_{\text{admitted }L}\log_2\!\left(\frac{L}{\max\!\left(2^{-r},\varepsilon^*(L)\right)}\right)\end{aligned}">family score = inf over admitted L of log2(L / max(2^(-r), ε*(L)))</div><p class="l-body">A proved bound gives a minimum score. A concrete colliding pair gives a maximum, using either an exact probability or a sampled estimate. <span id="search-star">In the chart, * marks a cap based on sampling.</span> The event counts and intervals describe uncertainty in that pair’s measured rate; a worse pair may still exist. A search that finds no collisions earns no score. Pairs that collide for every key can still have a positive length-adjusted cap when L &gt; 1; only such a pair at L = 1 caps the score at zero.</p><p class="l-body">For k observed collisions, the approximate 95% half-width is <span class="math" data-tex="\pm 2.83/\sqrt{k}">±2.83/√k</span> bits (= <span class="math" data-tex="\frac{1.96}{\sqrt{k}\,\ln 2}">1.96/(√k·ln 2)</span>); below about 35 events the interval is more than a bit wide. For the small-count rows, I use two-sided exact Poisson intervals, transformed through <span class="math" data-tex="\log_2(L/p)">log₂(L/p)</span>. These intervals describe the chosen witness. They give no interval for the unknown family score. Even the optimistic end of each small-count interval is more than 30 bits below the 64-bit reference.</p><p class="l-body">Here, <em>seed</em> means the small integer supplied to a hashing function; <em>key</em> means all the secret values its calculation uses. Turning one seed into many values does not make those values independently random. The <a href="#reader-glossary">terminology guide</a> explains the other terms used in the derivations.</p><p class="l-body">Every score here concerns the <b>full r-bit output</b>. Almost-universality alone is not preserved by truncation. <a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/1503.03465#page=2">Lemire–Kaser §2.1</a> explains why; the <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=37184076">nullc/Peters exchange</a> makes the practical point for Polymur. In the <a href="records/AUDIT_REVIEW.md#92-truncation-a-real-counterexample-and-a-valid-repair">audit’s counterexample</a>, full field residues never collide, but their low 64 bits collide for about <span class="math" data-tex="2^{-25}">2<sup>−25</sup></span> of keys. Table 2 marks each row’s masking scope. XOR-differential bounds (AXU), or additive-differential bounds (ADU) in the matching group, can justify a factor <span class="math" data-tex="2^{r-t}">2<sup>r−t</sup></span> for t retained bits. Ideal CLHASH/CLNH, simple tabulation, NH’s low bits and GHASH have such statements. ChainHash’s independent finalizer also gives a masking bound. Its old 64-bit bound must not be reused unchanged.</p><p class="l-body">Unshifted Horner is a useful warning. Change only the last message word by XOR <span class="math" data-tex="2^{63}">2<sup>63</sup></span>. That word is the constant coefficient, so the full outputs differ by exactly that bit for every key, while all low 63 bits agree. The positive-power polynomial is a different family with a differential theorem. Vector multiply-shift’s theorem selects the specified <em>high</em> bits of its wide accumulator. Polymur’s corrected certificate covers only the full output. An independent universal reduction to m buckets instead gives the general composition bound <span class="math" data-tex="\varepsilon + 1/m">ε + 1/m</span>.</p></section><section aria-labelledby="question" class="post-section l-page"><h2 class="l-body" id="question">What the tests measure</h2><p class="l-body">A statistical test can reveal unevenly distributed outputs, seeds that behave badly, or weak <em>avalanche behavior</em>—where changing a small part of the input fails to affect enough output bits. My experiment asks a different question: after fixing two distinct messages, how often do they collide as the secret seed changes?</p><p class="l-body">A hash can spread ordinary inputs well while treating specially related inputs identically. Some examples below lose the difference between messages before the secret can help. Others exploit relationships between parallel calculations, or collide only for a particular set of keys.</p><p class="l-body">Throughout this comparison, the messages are chosen before the hidden key. An attacker who can change later inputs after observing earlier hash values or timing has more information. A universal-hashing proof alone does not establish security in that setting, or make the hash suitable for authenticating messages.</p><p class="l-body">A uniform b-bit seed makes each fixed-pair probability an integer multiple of <span class="math" data-tex="2^{-b}">2<sup>−b</sup></span>. Any nonzero probability is at least that large and can be much larger. Where the input domain forces some collision for some key, this limits the worst-pair guarantee. It does not apply to an injective restricted domain. Output width alone supplies no guarantee; the natural random-function reference is 32 bits for a 32-bit output. The quarter-of-seeds and every-seed examples are structural failures beyond seed size.</p><p class="l-body">These probabilities range over independently drawn keys. Repeating the same pair under one process key does not amplify its collision chance. To assess a practical flood, I would need many distinct accepted keys and a target table with its encoding and bucket or probe model. I would also need an attack budget. Repeated insertion of one key usually finds or replaces an entry. Resizing with the same key and rehashing with a fresh key are different operations. The arbitrary-byte witnesses must survive the application’s input encoding.</p><p class="l-body">Observed collisions give an attacker more information. For an injectively encoded, single-key polynomial family of degree at most L, one observed full-output collision pins the evaluation key to at most L roots of a known nonzero difference polynomial. In linear families, colliding differences form a subspace and can be combined. A timing signal can reveal a collision even when the application never returns a hash. <a href="https://www.aumasson.jp/siphash/siphash.pdf#page=14">SipHash §7</a> gives this objection to a secret vector dot-product hash, including key recovery by Gaussian elimination. High-bit multiply-shift has its own theorem. A fixed-pair theorem alone gives no adaptive guarantee.</p><p class="l-body"><a href="https://www.aumasson.jp/siphash/siphash.pdf">SipHash</a> is the deployed answer to that stronger problem and the remedy proposed by the <a href="https://www.aumasson.jp/siphash/siphashdos_29c3_slides.pdf">29C3 work</a> cited below for its attacks. For example, <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/collections/struct.HashMap.html">Rust’s standard HashMap</a> documents SipHash-1-3 as its current default, and <a href="https://github.com/python/cpython/blob/3.11/Python/pyhash.c">CPython 3.11</a> supports the SipHash string-hash path. These are separate from choosing aHash or another hash here. For an adaptive attacker I would use a PRF such as SipHash, with its parameters and the application’s leakage in scope.</p></section>
