<section aria-labelledby="results" class="post-section l-page">
<h2 class="l-body" id="results">Claims and fixed-pair results</h2><p class="l-body">For each heuristic row, I report one specific pair. Its proved or measured collision rate gives a lower bound on the hash’s worst case, which caps the score. Only the proven rows give lower guarantees on strength.</p>
<p class="l-body">Table 1 gives the selected pairs, <a href="#metric">collision scores</a>, and two-host speeds from <a href="records/speeds.json">speeds.json</a>. The suite cells count <b>tests passed</b>. PASS covers the published configuration. FAIL means at least one check failed; it does not say whether the suite detected this collision. The count does not measure vulnerability severity. These historical suite runs are separate from the new <code>--test=Speed</code> runs. All Table 1 rows expose variable-length byte-string APIs. The last column gives the selected witness lengths. <a href="https://github.com/fwojcik/smhasher3/blob/3b619371047761408406991685da1c2b3f751899/README.md#goals-and-non-goals">SMHasher3’s documented non-goals</a> include an overall hash-quality score and a final ranking of hashes. Key size describes the collision experiment and can differ from a wrapper’s scalar seed.</p>
<p class="l-body">I quote excerpts; the surrounding documentation still matters. I use the badges to distinguish guidance or claims about collisions under an unknown key from statistical-quality claims and explicit security disclaimers. Qualitative guidance does not establish an AU theorem. CityHash, FarmHash and t1ha disclaim cryptographic suitability; MurmurHash explicitly disclaims intentional-collision resistance elsewhere. SpookyHash warns against an opponent. gxhash qualifies its safeguards. No violated keyed-security promise is implied for the statistical-quality rows.</p><p class="l-body">The separate historical <a href="verify/xxh3-64/README.md">32-byte pair A</a> gave 9, 12, 11 and 11 collisions per 2<sup>30</sup> keys in wyhash, rapidhash v1, rapidhash v3 and XXH3-64. Those counts do not describe the base-1143 pair scored here: its fresh XXH3-64 counts are 483 and 535 per 2<sup>30</sup> at 32 bytes. Search effort was unequal; these witness caps do not rank hashes. <a href="records/xxh3_32B_RESULT.md">Pair and count provenance</a>.</p><div aria-labelledby="heuristic-table-caption" class="table-scroll l-page" role="region" tabindex="0"><table id="heuristic-table"><caption id="heuristic-table-caption"><b>Table 1.</b> Fixed-pair results, sorted by the keyed combining step. Collision rates give lower bounds on the worst case; strength scores are upper caps. Exact caps round upward; sampled caps carry event counts and intervals. <sup>*</sup> is the search marker defined below. Bulk speed uses 262144-byte inputs. A dash means no matching measurement.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Hash / result credit</th><th scope="col">Family</th><th scope="col">Official wording</th><th scope="col">Key / output bits</th><th scope="col">Suite result: tests passed/run</th><th scope="col">M2 Pro bulk B/cycle</th><th scope="col">Xeon 8375C bulk B/cycle</th><th scope="col">M2 Pro 1–31 B, cycles/hash</th><th scope="col">Xeon 8375C 1–31 B, cycles/hash</th><th scope="col">Collisi