"#appendix-siphash">SipHash-1-3</a><small class="cell-note">comparison</small></td><td class="family-cell" title="ARX permutation keyed by a 128-bit key (PRF).">ARX PRF</td><td><a href="https://www.aumasson.jp/siphash/siphash.pdf">“a fast short-input PRF”</a></td><td>128 / 64</td><td>not supplied</td><td><span title="SipHash-1-3; backend token: portable; median of all five runs; run spread 25.00%">1.13</span><sup><a href="#arm-backend-note" title="No dedicated ARM backend in this SMHasher3 tree">†</a></sup></td><td><span title="SipHash-1-3; backend token: ssse3; best of two runs; run spread 0.00%">1.01</span></td><td><span title="SipHash-1-3; backend token: portable; median of all five runs; run spread 24.99%">50.39</span><sup><a href="#arm-backend-note" title="No dedicated ARM backend in this SMHasher3 tree">†</a></sup></td><td><span title="SipHash-1-3; backend token: ssse3; best of two runs; run spread 0.44%">70.58</span></td><td>no pair found</td><td>no theorem, no pair found: no score</td><td>No pair found; no score</td></tr><tr data-hash-id="siphash-2-4"><td><a href="#appendix-siphash">SipHash-2-4</a><small class="cell-note">comparison</small></td><td class="family-cell" title="ARX permutation keyed by a 128-bit key (PRF).">ARX PRF</td><td><a href="https://www.aumasson.jp/siphash/siphash.pdf">“a fast short-input PRF”</a></td><td>128 / 64</td><td>not supplied</td><td><span title="SipHash-2-4; backend token: portable; best of two runs; run spread 0.00%">0.59</span><sup><a href="#arm-backend-note" title="No dedicated ARM backend in this SMHasher3 tree">†</a></sup></td><td><span title="SipHash-2-4; backend token: ssse3; best of two runs; run spread 0.00%">0.53</span></td><td><span title="SipHash-2-4; backend token: portable; best of two runs; run spread 0.15%">73.41</span><sup><a href="#arm-backend-note" title="No dedicated ARM backend in this SMHasher3 tree">†</a></sup></td><td><span title="SipHash-2-4; backend token: ssse3; best of two runs; run spread 0.15%">95.55</span></td><td>no pair found</td><td>no theorem, no pair found: no score</td><td>No pair found; no score</td></tr><tr data-hash-id="halftime24"><td><a href="#appendix-halftime24">HalftimeHash24 (advanced 24-byte API, Encode3)</a><small class="cell-note">NEW</small></td><td class="family-cell" title="NH keyed pair products, encoding and tree combination; advanced Encode3 core.">NH trees</td><td><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.08865">“For HalftimeHash24, and for strings less than an exabyte in length, this is more than 83 bits of entropy”</a></td><td>full key array / 192</td><td>not timed / no suite supplied</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>every key (unequal lengths; the raw core is fixed-length by design); at equal length, collides for at least 1 of every 2^32 keys (counted); sampled 76/316053236830, 95% interval [2^-32.30, 2^-31.63]</td><td>0 bits, key-free (unequal lengths; the raw core is fixed-length by design); at most 36.4 bits at equal length (verified 2^-32 pair)</td><td>0/1 B; L = 1<br/><a href="#appendix-halftime24">Pair + key</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="l-body" id="arm-backend-note">† No dedicated ARM hash backend exists in this SMHasher3 tree; generic code may still use shared ARM arithmetic helpers. I retain the backend tokens verbatim. A blank means no named token; UMASH/CLHASH’s <code>hwclmul</code> token runs PMULL on ARM, and aHash’s <code>portable</code> names its shuffle while its AES rounds use ARM instructions. Hover a speed for its token and run spread.</p><p class="l-body"><b>The “family” column groups hashes by the operations they use.</b> A lane is one of several parallel calculations. Constant-multiplier designs mix the state with fixed numbers; multiply-fold designs compute a large product and combine its two halves. ARX means addition, bit rotation and XOR. AES-based designs reuse encryption instructions for mixing. The narrow-multiply labels specify the sizes of the integers being multiplied. These labels explain structure, not safety: the collision analysis must still follow how the secret and message interact. The linked appendix entries spell out those steps.</p><p class="l-body"><b>Coverage of rurban’s recommended list.</b> All sixteen entries in <a href="https://rurban.github.io/smhasher/">rurban’s list</a> have a corresponding family here. The muted aliases show the mapping. Tested versions and key distributions can differ. <code>rapidhash</code> maps to v1/v3; <code>xxh3low</code> to XXH3-64; <code>wyhash</code> to final v4.3; <code>umash</code> to UMASH-64/-128 in Table 2; <code>ahash64</code> to the specified aHash AES path; <code>t1ha2_atonce</code> to t1ha2-64; <code>a5hash</code> to its 64-bit row; <code>komihash</code> and <code>FarmHash</code> to their named rows. <code>halftime_hash128</code> is the Style128 wrapper with 64-bit output in Table 2; <code>Spooky32</code> maps to SpookyHash V2; <code>pengyhash</code>, <code>nmhash32</code> and <code>mx3</code> map to their rows; <code>MUM/mir</code> maps to both; <code>fasthash32</code> maps to fasthash-32 and its 64-bit parent. Rurban’s pengyhash v0.2 and mx3 v1.0.0 differ from the v0.3/v3 rows here. Foldhash was added because it is the <a href="https://github.com/rust-lang/hashbrown/blob/v0.16.1/README.md#L29-L30">hashbrown crate’s default hasher (README, lines 29–30)</a>. Rust’s standard <code>HashMap</code> uses <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/collections/struct.HashMap.html">SipHash-1-3</a>.</p><p class="l-body"><a href="https://github.com/rurban/smhasher/blob/master/Hashes.h#L408"><code>xxh3low</code></a> takes the low 32 bits of XXH3-64. SpookyHash’s <a href="https://github.com/rurban/smhasher/blob/master/SpookyV2.h"><code>Hash32</code></a> returns a truncation of its shared 128-bit core; its native seed is 32 bits, so the probability still needs that seed model. <a href="https://github.com/rurban/smhasher/blob/master/fasthash.cpp"><code>fasthash32</code></a> computes <code>h − (h &gt;&gt; 32)</code> from fasthash64. Full-output equality therefore survives those reductions. Rurban’s <a href="https://github.com/tkaitchuck/aHash/blob/v0.8.12/smhasher/ahash-cbindings/src/lib.rs">aHash binding</a> calls <code>RandomState::with_seeds(seed, seed, seed, seed).hash_one(&amp;buf)</code> on a byte slice. Its native-CPU build selects AES on an AES-NI host. That correlated seed wrapper is separate from this post’s four-independent-word collision experiment. No custom-secret XXH3 or wyhash collision rate is established here.</p>
<p class="l-body">The paper-family rows retain their <b>REPRODUCTION</b> credits. Most supplied estimates use <span class="math" data-tex="2^{30}">2<sup>30</sup></span> trials; the updated XXH3-64 pair uses two fresh runs pooled to <span class="math" data-tex="2^{31}">2<sup>31</sup></span>. MUM’s every-seed check uses <span class="math" data-tex="2^{20}">2<sup>20</sup></span> trials per tested unroll variant. I use public constants and a random 64-bit API seed for these rows. Peters’ wyhash work concerns fixed-seed multicollisions; the seeded fold differential is credited to <a href="https://github.com/thomasahle/fast-polynomials">the paper</a>.</p>
<p class="l-body">Across all thirty rows, twenty have FAIL suite results, six PASS and four have no supplied verdict; the wyhash suite result is for v4.2, as labeled. HighwayHash’s PASS covers 238 applicable tests rather than the 250-test set. Each all-seed row that FAILs has at least one Seed* failure. Other fixed-pair failures escape those tests: aHash fails only Sparse and TwoBytes. Passing Seed* also gives no certificate: MuseAir v0.3 and a5hash-128 pass all 250 tests and collide for every seed. Seed* tests vary seeds under fixed inputs; related-seed events differ from the fixed-pair, same-hidden-seed event scored here.</p><p class="l-body" id="disclosure"><b>Disclosure.</b> The new heuristic issue drafts and other proof-author notes await my review. I claim no response to an unsent draft. UMASH’s proof question was opened as <a href="https://github.com/backtrace-labs/umash/issues/40">issue #40</a> on 2026-09-18. The earlier <a href="https://github.com/Cyan4973/xxHash/issues/1127">xxHash report #1127</a> was opened on 2 September. In his <a href="https://github.com/Cyan4973/xxHash/issues/1127#issuecomment-5720905442">17 September reply</a>, maintainer Cyan4973 (Yann Collet) thanked the reporter for the report and reproduction, described “higher-than-brute-force chances” for carefully crafted pairs in the 32–240-byte range at “1 in 100 millions approximately”, said collision resistance is outside XXH3’s noncryptographic guarantees, and said the <code>XXH3_mix16B()</code> comment will be updated to document the finding.</p>
<p class="l-body">In the examples, <code>hex("...")</code> means decode literal bytes, excluding quotation marks and any terminating zero byte (NUL). Adjacent strings concatenate. The snippets use C-like notation to specify inputs; <a href="data.json">data.json</a> also contains every complete byte string, including the long ones. Each displayed example seed demonstrates a collision. For all-seed statements, I also rely on the structural arguments or enumerations described below.</p>
</section>
<section aria-labelledby="mechanisms" class="post-section l-page"><h2 class="l-body" id="mechanisms">How the pairs collide</h2><p class="l-body">Several hashes lose the distinction between messages through arithmetic that an attacker can predict without the seed. This explains the every-seed examples in <a href="#appendix-cityhash64">CityHash</a>, <a href="#appendix-farmhash64">FarmHash</a>, <a href="#appendix-gxhash">gxhash</a>, <a href="#appendix-museair">MuseAir v0.3</a>, <a href="#appendix-pengyhash">pengyhash</a>, <a href="#appendix-mx3">mx3</a>, <a href="#appendix-mir">mir</a> and <a href="#appendix-fasthash">fasthash</a>. Later secret-dependent processing receives identical values and cannot distinguish the inputs.</p><p class="l-body">Other constructions introduce a difference early in the calculation and cancel it later. That works for every seed in the selected <a href="#appendix-murmurhash3">MurmurHash3</a> and <a href="#appendix-nmhash32x">nmhash32x</a> pairs. For <a href="#appendix-spookyhash">SpookyHash</a>, <a href="#appendix-ahash">aHash</a>, <a href="#appendix-nmhash32">nmhash32</a>, <a href="#appendix-t1ha2">t1ha2</a> and <a href="#appendix-highwayhash">HighwayHash</a>, the cancellation instead requires particular key values. Counting or sampling those keys gives the reported probabilities.</p><p class="l-body">In <a href="#appendix-a5hash-64">a5hash-64</a>, some seeds make a multiplication erase the message difference. The <a href="#appendix-a5hash-128">128-bit variant</a> has a separate pair for which that erasure happens for every seed. The worked komihash example below shows another pattern: parallel calculations with related secrets can be made to cancel each other. <a href="#appendix-a">Appendix A</a> gives the remaining derivations.</p><p class="l-body">A trail specifies a sequence of internal differences. To prove a collision, its conditions must imply equality of the full output. For a class C enabling a trail and its additional success event E, <span class="math" data-tex="\Pr[C\cap E] = \Pr[C]\Pr[E\mid C]">Pr[C ∩ E] = Pr[C] Pr[E | C]</span>. Distinct rounds do not make events independent. Excluding one enabling class removes that contribution; it does not prove the remaining keys safe.</p><p class="l-body patterns-pointer">The <a href="#recurring-patterns">recurring patterns</a> at the start of Appendix A group these examples by the arithmetic that makes them collide. Each pattern links to its affected entries and explains what a proof would need to rule out.</p></section>
<section aria-labelledby="lanes" class="post-section l-page">
<h3 class="l-body" id="lanes">komihash: related lanes are not independent keys</h3>
<p class="l-body">Komihash divides a message among parallel calculations, called <em>lanes</em>. Their starting values depend on the secret, but are related by known constants. An attacker can compensate for those relationships in the message without knowing the secret itself. The example below shows how this makes two changes almost always cancel.</p>
<p class="l-body"><b>komihash — NEW.</b> The selected pair differs in just the low bit of two words:</p>
<pre class="l-page" tabindex="0"><code class="c">/* Hex encodes bytes in memory order, not hexadecimal integers. */
A = hex("0000000000000000447370032e8a191311111111111111112222222222222222"
    "f0f9dda4c1c0a15e9cf534900ea6f5e033333333333333334444444444444444");
B = hex("0100000000000000457370032e8a191311111111111111112222222222222222"
    "f0f9dda4c1c0a15e9cf534900ea6f5e033333333333333334444444444444444");

/* Example seed: */
0x27d1f77dc2a01269
/* Both 64-bit hashes: 0x113c6b88bc913857 */</code></pre>
<p class="l-body">I measured a rate of about 0.9106 over uniform 64-bit seeds. The large recorded run found 3,910,946,997 collisions in <span class="math" data-tex="2^{32}">2<sup>32</sup></span> trials; the second model family independently measured 0.9106. Randomizing the ordinary API seed leaves this result intact.</p>
<p class="l-body">The initialization in the checked <a href="https://github.com/avaneev/komihash/blob/5.34/komihash.h">v5.34 source record</a> is:</p>
<pre class="l-page" tabindex="0"><code class="c">uint64_t Seed1 = KOMIHASH_IVAL1 ^ ( UseSeed &amp; KOMIHASH_VAL01 );
uint64_t Seed5 = KOMIHASH_IVAL5 ^ ( UseSeed &amp; KOMIHASH_VAL10 );</code></pre>
<p class="l-body">After preseeding, the long-input path constructs additional lane states by XORing public constants into the resulting secret words. The message can compensate for those same constants. For the displayed pair, two lanes perform identical products within each message. Their low halves cancel in the XOR fold. Flipping the selected bits changes the common high product by at most one; often it does not change at all, and many remaining cases cancel through the additions as well.</p>
<p class="l-body">The ordinary seed expansion makes the relevant high state nonuniform. Tuning the common message word exploits that distribution and raises the rate. Seed-dependent operands still expose relations when lanes inherit public differences. An attacker can arrange related operands without calculating their secret values.</p>
<p class="l-body">The 0.9106 result applies to this construction at lengths <b>64 through 127 bytes only</b>, with identical suffixes. At those lengths, the lanes fold immediately after the first large block. At 128 bytes, another block processes the still-distinct lanes before that fold. I found no collisions there at the tested resolution. This gives no proof of zero collision probability and no bound for arbitrary longer inputs. The SMHasher3 v5.27 core and the checked v5.34 implementation agree for this analysis.</p>
<p class="l-body">With <span class="math" data-tex="L=8">L = 8</span>, this is an estimated score cap of <b>3.14 bits</b>; <a href="#appendix-komihash">Appendix A gives the numbered pseudo-code and reproduction details</a>.</p>
</section><section aria-labelledby="many-keys" class="post-section l-page"><h3 class="l-body" id="many-keys">Collisions among many keys</h3><p class="l-body">For n fixed distinct keys chosen independently of the hash key, a pairwise <span class="math" data-tex="\varepsilon">ε</span>-AU bound gives an expected number of colliding pairs at most <span class="math" data-tex="\varepsilon\binom{n}{2}">ε·C(n,2)</span>, by linearity of expectation. It does not require independent pair events. Use a common length cap that covers every pair, or sum their individual bounds. A fixed-pair result does not by itself give an n-way multicollision or a latency-tail bound.</p><p class="l-body">Some of these mechanisms generate more than a pair. <a href="verify/cityhash-64/README.md">CityHash64 pair B</a> analytically inverts HashLen17to32 and gives a <span class="math" data-tex="2^{192}">2<sup>192</sup></span> set of 32-byte messages with the same unseeded value, hence equality for every seed. <a href="verify/farmhash-64/README.md">FarmHash64’s corresponding inversion</a> also generates equal-length collisions. The <a href="verify/murmurhash3-128/README.md">32-byte MurmurHash3 construction</a> cancels the state difference to (0, 0), so interchangeable blocks compose into multicollisions. The absolute state need not reset; the difference is what cancels. The <a href="verify/museair/README.md">MuseAir construction</a> adjusts the head by <span class="math" data-tex="P(T)\oplus P(T\prime)">P(T) XOR P(T′)</span> for arbitrary tails of a fixed allowed length, giving many distinct messages with the same pre-seed state. The <a href="verify/gxhash-64/README.md">gxhash construction</a> gives each message shorter than 16 bytes a 16-byte twin; this generates pairs without establishing arbitrary arity in one collision class.</p><p class="l-body">The new komihash, SpookyHash, aHash, t1ha2, a5hash, HighwayHash, pengyhash, nmhash32/32x, mx3 and fasthash results are fixed-pair results here; mir extends the public-fold mechanism. The verification records check the stated pairs, classes and some common-suffix consequences. They do not establish an arbitrary-arity generator for these rows. Appending the same suffix to a state collision keeps a pair colliding; it does not alone prove that independently chosen collisions compose. I have not established whether the high-probability komihash construction composes.</p></section>
<section aria-labelledby="proofs" class="post-section l-page"><h2 class="l-body" id="proofs">What a proof buys</h2><p class="l-body">A proof replaces “we did not find a bad pair” with a statement covering every allowed pair. One way to achieve this is <em>polynomial hashing</em>: turn each message into a distinct polynomial, then evaluate it at a secret random value. Two different polynomials can agree at only a limited number of values. Counting those possible agreements gives a collision bound. Other constructions in the table use different algebraic arguments to reach the same kind of guarantee.</p><p class="l-body">In <b>Table 2</b>, I use the <a href="records/AUDIT.md">proof audit</a> and its <a href="records/AUDIT_REVIEW.md">second reading</a>, including the corrections they establish. The status column records which arguments are complete and which published steps remain unresolved. An unresolved proof step alone gives no collision attack. No collision violating the claimed rate is known for production UMASH or the shipped HalftimeHash wrappers. UMASH’s claimed numbers keep hollow markers; the certified UMASH-64 fallback is now 46.52 bits, and UMASH-128 inherits the corrected linear envelope. The corrected HalftimeHash wrapper bound and the refuted advanced API are described below.</p><div aria-labelledby="proof-table-caption" class="table-scroll l-page" role="region" tabindex="0"><table id="proof-table"><caption id="proof-table-caption"><b>Table 2.</b> Audited bounds and implementation costs. Scores minimize <a href="#metric">The collision score</a> over the stated domain; displayed guarantees round downward. Guarantees require each row’s stated key distribution. Timings do not certify seed expansion. HalftimeHash’s four values follow style order 64, 128, 256, 512; every style returns 64 bits.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Hash</th><th scope="col">Family</th><th scope="col">Claimed / proved ε bound (q = 2^64)</th><th scope="col">Score / minimizing L</t