</tbody></table></div><p class="l-body" id="pinned-bound-note">The “bound at 1 GB” column evaluates each row’s own formula. I show −log₂ of that upper bound, rounded to four decimal places where needed; an approximation is marked ≈. Hover or open a value for its formula and scope. Fixed-length rows compare equal-length messages. Table 2 uses ChainHash model A and the full HalftimeHash wrapper bound. CLHASH uses its sharper stated length-specific formula; the length-independent 2.004/2<sup>64</sup> envelope would give about 62.9971 bits.</p><p class="l-body" id="umash-length-note">UMASH-128 needs a special length qualification. Its claimed bound is <span class="math" data-tex="\lceil L/2^{23}\rceil^2\cdot2^{-83}">⌈L/2²³⌉² · 2⁻⁸³</span>, giving a claimed score of at least <span class="math" data-tex="\min(83+\log_2 L,127-\log_2 L)">min(83 + log₂L, 127 − log₂L)</span>. The displayed 83-bit claim holds up to 2<sup>46</sup> eight-byte words, about 512 TiB. The claimed score falls to about 68 bits over the full 64-bit length range. The quadratic claim remains neither proved nor refuted regardless of length; the corrected proof supplies only the inherited linear envelope ε ≤ 45635·⌈L/512⌉/2<sup>61</sup>.</p><p class="l-body"><b>The “family” column describes how a hash combines message pieces with a key.</b> Polynomial hashes evaluate a message-dependent polynomial at a secret value. A prime-field polynomial uses arithmetic modulo a prime; GF(2<sup>k</sup>) uses a different arithmetic on k-bit values. NH adds secret values to pairs of words, multiplies them and combines the products; PH is a related construction using carry-less multiplication, where bit products combine without ordinary integer carries. Tabulation uses independently random lookup tables. Multiply-shift multiplies by a secret value and selects specified high bits. Labels joined by “+” describe successive stages: for example, a fast block hash followed by a polynomial that combines the blocks. The appendix gives the exact arithmetic and parameters for each row.</p><section aria-labelledby="proof-gaps" class="post-section l-page"><h3 class="l-body" id="proof-gaps">Two proofs with gaps</h3><p class="l-body">The published 55-bit and 83-bit claims for <a href="#appendix-umash">UMASH</a> are neither proved nor refuted. An unconditional corrected bound now certifies a 46.52-bit score for UMASH-64; UMASH-128 inherits only its linear envelope. The ENH-only case (<code>OpenENHOnly</code>) is closed with joint collision probability &lt;2<sup>−105</sup>; the sharp primary projection constant and the PH+ENH case (<code>OpenPHENH</code>) remain open. The <a href="records/umash-corrected/PROOF.md">natural-language proof</a> passed three independent adversarial reviews and exhaustive scaled checks; the <a href="records/umash-corrected/VERDICT.md">verified judgement</a> records the scope. No production collision violating the claimed rate is known.</p><p class="l-body">For <a href="#appendix-halftimehash">HalftimeHash’s public 64-bit functions</a>, a corrected argument establishes a 63-bit guarantee within explicit length limits and under the stated assumptions about program execution. Those points are filled. Its separate <a href="#appendix-halftime24">advanced function with a 24-byte output</a> has a counterexample to the original claim. A repaired version has its own entry. These are different functions, so a result about one should not be transferred to the others.</p></section><section aria-labelledby="key-provenance" class="post-section l-page"><h3 class="l-body" id="key-provenance">Choosing the key</h3><p class="l-body">The <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=17767153">2018 discussion of universal hashing</a> asked a practical question: where should an implementation get its random key values? The table below compares how much key material each construction keeps in memory, measured in 64-bit words. It follows <a href="data.json">the source data</a> and <a href="records/AUDIT.md">audit</a>. A larger key is not automatically more secure; the proof must explain how the algorithm uses it.</p><ol class="l-body"><li><b>One uniform word.</b> Horner and BRW each need one uniform field word, so a full-width 64-bit seed can preserve their theorems. Their separate paper-harness speeds are 1.30 and 6.00 GB/s at 16 KB. Their fixed-length and full-output restrictions still apply.</li><li><b>A key object drawn once.</b> The injective recurrence uses three words, or four with lane combination. Polymur has four resident words, including derived values; its evaluation key must be uniform on the restricted set K. ChainHash model A draws 10 independent words, 80 bytes, and expands the PH seed into field powers; its resident expanded schedule remains 41 words (328 bytes). UMASH has a 38-word schedule, and CLHASH has 133 resident words with the specified restrictions on its polynomial key. Draw the required randomness once from the OS CSPRNG and use the theorem’s exact sampling rule, with rejection where needed. This supplies the stated key distribution without a short-seed expansion assumption. The information-theoretic statement requires that specified uniform distribution.</li><li><b>Keys that grow or occupy substantial cache.</b> NH and CLNH need <span class="math" data-tex="2\lceil L/2\rceil">2 ceil(L/2)</span> words; vector multiply-shift needs <span class="math" data-tex="2(L+1)">2(L+1)</span> because its coefficients are 128-bit. Simple tabulation needs <span class="math" data-tex="256c">256c</span> words for c byte positions. HalftimeHash’s shipped wrapper needs 70,928 bytes, or 69.3 KiB, exceeding a typical L1 data cache.</li></ol><p class="l-body">Rust’s BuildHasher can carry a large key object; aHash itself carries four words. Fixed built-in interfaces such as Go’s maphash, Python’s object-hash slot and Java’s hashCode are a different integration problem. Object hashing also needs an encoding and composition rule that preserve distinctions, including lengths and structure. A byte-string theorem does not supply those automatically.</p><p class="l-body">A 64-bit seed can choose at most 2<sup>64</sup> keys, however many bytes the initializer produces. That changes the random experiment assumed by a proof requiring a much larger independent key. A cryptographic random generator may support a separate computational argument; using SplitMix64 does not supply such an argument here. ChainHash’s original <em>model A</em> is an explicitly proved setup: start with 80 random bytes and derive the remaining values by the specified arithmetic. The benchmark uses a different initializer, outside the timed hashing loop. I have no setup timing for an initializer that meets the theorem’s assumptions.</p></section><h3 class="l-body" id="proof-audit-notes">What the audit certifies</h3><p class="l-body">The audited bounds cover ideal CLHASH/carryless NH (CLNH), NH, full-field polynomial hashing, simple tabulation, vector multiply-shift, the paper’s recurrence and ChainHash. Bernstein–Rabin–Winograd (BRW), unshifted Horner, both recurrence rows, CLNH, NH, multiply-shift and tabulation retain their fixed-length restrictions. One-word padding for NH/CLNH is a convention within each fixed-length family, not a proof for unequal lengths. The recurrence rows also require the stated injective field-word encoding. The <a href="#key-provenance">key provenance</a> and Table 2’s masking column are part of each guarantee.</p><p class="l-body">The bound variable is L in eight-byte words. Except where fixed-length restrictions apply, the audited byte domain is shorter than <span class="math" data-tex="2^{64}">2<sup>64</sup></span> bytes. BRW’s loose envelope reaches its finite-domain minimum at <span class="math" data-tex="L = 2^{61}-1">L = 2<sup>61</sup>−1</span>. The other audited plotted rows attain their minimum at <span class="math" data-tex="L=1">L = 1</span>. Vector multiply-shift needs 128-bit coefficients, so the resident key uses <span class="math" data-tex="2(L+1)">2(L+1)</span> 64-bit words.</p><p class="l-body"><b>PolymurHash 2.0.</b> The second reading resolves the ideal-key cardinality gap: <span class="math" data-tex="|K| \ge K_0 = 189729088763903999">|K| ≥ K0 = 189729088763903999</span>. With the sharper degree bound <span class="math" data-tex="D(8L)/K_0">D(8L)/K0</span>, the minimum is <span class="math" data-tex="\log_2(K_0/9) = 54.2267934975\ldots">log₂(K0/9) = 54.2267934975…</span> at <span class="math" data-tex="L=1">L = 1</span>. For n bytes, <span class="math" data-tex="D(n)">D(n)</span> is 2 through 7 bytes, 9 through 21, 13 through 49, and <span class="math" data-tex="7\lfloor(n-1)/49\rfloor+14">7 floor((n−1)/49)+14</span> thereafter. I could not complete the headline constant or almost strongly universal (ASU) argument as written. The conservative almost universal (AU) bound is <span class="math" data-tex="D(n)/K_0">D(n)/K0</span> under a uniform key in K. The shipped initializer’s distribution needs a separate bound.</p><p class="l-body"><a href="#appendix-umash">UMASH’s appendix entry</a> records the corrected status and theorem scope, with independent checks.</p><p class="l-body"><a href="#appendix-halftimehash">HalftimeHash’s appendix entry</a> records the corrected status and theorem scope, with independent checks.</p><p class="l-body">The review also resolves the 32-bit UHASH collision bound in Request for Comments (RFC) 4418 and the revised VHASH theorem. It does not establish UHASH’s advertised higher-output powers. A conservative joint bound is <span class="math" data-tex="U_t = a+(1-a)b^t">U<sub>t</sub> = a+(1−a)b<sup>t</sup></span>, where <span class="math" data-tex="a = 2^{-32}">a = 2<sup>−32</sup></span> and <span class="math" data-tex="b = 2^{-35}+\frac{2^{54}+6}{2^{100}}+\frac{32(2^{28}+1)}{2^{64}}">b = 2<sup>−35</sup>+(2<sup>54</sup>+6)/2<sup>100</sup>+32(2<sup>28</sup>+1)/2<sup>64</sup></span>. <span class="math" data-tex="U_1">U<sub>1</sub></span> scores 30.3561; <span class="math" data-tex="t=2,3,4">t = 2, 3, 4</span> score just below 32. These are hash-equality bounds, not MAC-forgery bounds.</p><p class="l-body"><a href="#appendix-chainhash">ChainHash’s appendix entry</a> records the corrected status and theorem scope, with independent checks.</p><p class="l-body">Prose audits and machine-checked theorems have distinct scopes. The <a href="records/LEAN_STATUS.md">base Lean status</a> covers polynomial hashing over a general field, ZMod p and GF(2<sup>64</sup>), field NH/CLNH, simple tabulation, the three-key recurrence with n/|F|, and conditional ChainHash composition. The newer <a href="#appendix-chainhash">concrete ChainHash reference theorem</a>, <a href="records/polymur-card/LEAN_POLYMUR_STATUS.md">unconditional numerical Polymur result</a> and <a href="records/halftime/LEAN_HALFTIME_STATUS.md">abstract HalftimeHash model</a> are recorded separately; Table 2’s Lean column identifies that scope.</p><p class="l-body">The pair-collision score does not summarize independence, concentration or a collision-resolution scheme’s probe-length theorem. Alongside polynomial hashing, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.01523">tabulation also has guarantees tailored to particular algorithms</a>. I would choose the property the data structure requires.</p></section>
<section aria-labelledby="speed" class="post-section l-page"><h2 class="l-body" id="speed">The cost of a guarantee</h2><p class="l-body">On Apple M2 Pro, I measured ChainHash (paper), 256 B blocks, at 22.80 B/cycle, rapidhash v3 15.03 and XXH3-64 13.16; the retained Xeon values are 14.42, 10.67 and 19.69. These measurements time the hashing core. They exclude whole-table lookups.</p><p class="l-body"><b>Short keys expose the fixed cost.</b> At 1–31 bytes, ChainHash (paper) costs 70.49 cycles/hash on M2, versus 21.08 for rapidhash v3, 25.05 for komihash, 24.51 for XXH3-64, and 32.86 for PolymurHash. On Xeon the corresponding figures are 103.90, 27.58, 27.49, 30.16, and 39.16. ChainHash’s degree-five finalizer performs three serial field multiply-and-reduce stages; its setup is outside the timed loop. </p><p class="l-body">Each primary host pass uses one binary from the same SMHasher3 source snapshot; the corrected M2 binary also includes the added registrations. The ARM detection fix makes <code>family.cmake</code> accept <code>arm64</code>. XXH3 moves scalar→NEON, HighwayHash and HalftimeHash portable→NEON, gxhash generic portable→ARM AES, and aHash’s AES rounds now use ARM instructions although its shuffle token remains <code>portable</code>. ChainHash’s PMULL path was already enabled; NMHASH/NMHASHX and SipHash have no dedicated ARM SIMD backend here. UMASH and CLHASH are now registered through ARM PMULL adapters. I retain the old Xeon pass, with SipHash additions from its original binary and the classical wrappers from the documented separate OpenSSL-enabled Xeon build. <a href="records/m2_REPORT.md">Build/backend audit</a> · <a href="records/speeds.json">merged records</a>.</p><p class="l-body">Bulk is the fixed 262144-byte alignment average; short keys average 1–31 bytes. For most rows, I select the better of two runs independently per metric. For eight retimed M2 hashes, I use the median of all five runs. The three added runs used the same binary, with the load gate relaxed from &lt;3.0 to &lt;4.5 on 12 logical CPUs. I retain the two calibration outliers (ChainHash-256 run 1: 31.42 B/cycle; ChainHash-1k run 1: 48.46) in both the median and spread. The <a href="records/speeds_m2_v2.json">supplied v2 records</a> contain those medians; no outlier was discarded. The retiming log records the relaxed gate; the input metadata describes the original &lt;3.0 target. Every seeded wrapper in this benchmark samples a seed-defined subfamily: a 64-bit seed supplies at most 2<sup>64</sup> members, so the ideal independent-key bound does not apply without a separate theorem for that sampler. New fixed HalftimeHash timings use three-run medians on both hosts; new ChainHash measurements use the protocols linked in their rows.</p><p class="l-body">M2 run-to-run spread, defined as 100(max/min−1), across the merged registrations: bulk maximum 173.79%, median 2.94%; short-key maximum 158.80%, median 2.33%. Spreads include every retained run, including calibration outliers; individual cell titles report the row spread.</p><p class="l-body">Xeon run-to-run spread, defined as 100(max/min−1), across the merged registrations: bulk maximum 1.86%, median 0.00%; short-key maximum 1.08%, median 0.15%. Spreads include every retained run, including calibration outliers; individual cell titles report the row spread.</p><p class="l-body">The M2 has 12 physical/logical cores. The initial corrected pass’s boundary load1 ranged 2.88–27.80 after a start gate at 2.92. Xeon has 48 physical cores / 96 logical CPUs in a KVM guest; the retained old pass recorded load1 2.16–18.34, while the SipHash additions recorded 9.45–10.13 and were pinned to CPU 6 after sampling idle siblings. I use no affinity adjustment on M2. M2’s cycle unit is a calibrated monotonic-clock estimate; Xeon uses TSC ticks. SMHasher3’s GiB/s conversion assumes 3.5 GHz on both; the two units cannot be treated as interchangeable physical core-cycle measurements.</p><p class="l-body">Poly1305 and GHASH were timed through OpenSSL 3 optimized code as added SMHasher3 registrations: EVP_MAC POLY1305 and GMAC. The GHASH figure is a GMAC proxy that adds an AES block, AES key setup and a seed-dependent output mask per call; Poly1305 also includes seed expansion and setup, so short-key costs include these overheads. Their uniform-key mathematical bounds do not certify the benchmark seed expanders. <a href="records/classic_REPORT.md">Wrapper and control records</a>.</p><p class="l-body" id="benchmark-verification">‡ The corrected pass retains pre-existing implementation verification-code failures for NMHASH/NMHASHX (scalar M2 values differ from Xeon) and four poly-mersenne registrations. All passed the six structural Sanity checks; the six code failures remain marked in the records. Timing is not a cross-host correctness certificate.</p><p class="l-body">The paper harness reports separate measurements in decimal GB/s: ChainHash-256 at 67.7 GB/s for 16 KB and 37.6 for 512 B, XXH3 at 38.2 GB/s for 16 KB, and Horner sequential/unrolled at 1.3/5.1 GB/s. I do not use these as SMHasher3 cells.</p></section>
<section aria-labelledby="choice" class="post-section l-page"><h2 class="l-body" id="choice">The choice I would make</h2><p class="l-body">Before choosing a hash for a table, I would check three things: how the program turns its inputs into bytes, how it generates the secret key, and how it maps the output to table slots. A guarantee is useful only if it covers those choices. For inputs chosen without feedback about the secret, the tradeoffs in this study are:</p><ul class="l-body"><li><b>Portable code and short inputs:</b> PolymurHash has a corrected guarantee of at least 54.2267 bits for its full output and takes 32.86 cycles per short hash on M2, or 39.16 on Xeon. Its key generator and any reduction to fewer output bits need separate justification.</li>
<li><b>Long inputs on x86 with carry-less multiplication instructions:</b> CLHASH combines a 64-bit guarantee under its stated random-key assumptions with 11.56 bytes per cycle on Xeon. The theorem applies to the paper’s Algorithm 4, so check that the implementation and key setup match it.</li>
<li><b>Long inputs on ARM64:</b> My original ChainHash gives a 61-bit guarantee with 80 independently random key bytes and reaches 22.80 bytes per cycle on M2. Its short-input cost is higher: 70.49 cycles per hash, versus Polymur’s 32.86 and rapidhash v3’s 21.08.</li>
<li><b>An attacker who learns from earlier requests:</b> A cryptographic keyed hash such as SipHash addresses a stronger setting. Account for what outputs or timing the application reveals. SipHash-1-3 and 2-4 take 50.39 and 73.41 cycles per short hash on M2, or 70.58 and 95.55 on Xeon. Neither receives a collision score in this post.</li>
<li><b>A pair that collides for every key:</b> Changing the key cannot separate that pair in the stated construction.</li></ul><p class="l-body">Detection, bounded fallback and logging remain useful where the data structure supports them. They cannot supply a missing collision theorem. Reseeding leaves the komihash pair colliding for about 91.06% of fresh seeds.</p><p class="l-body">I found the gap in UMASH’s proof, the gap and refutation in HalftimeHash’s, and the collisions in the heuristic hashes. I used independent execution and proof reviews to check the witnesses and separate failed arguments from corrected guarantees.</p><p class="l-body">Several mathematical arguments have also been turned into proofs that Lean can check. Completed checks cover <a href="records/chainhash-integrate/STATUS.md">the original ChainHash construction and its 80-byte key setup</a>, <a href="records/chainhash128/STATUS.md">ChainHash-128 and its 160-byte setup</a>, <a href="records/polymur-card/LEAN_POLYMUR_STATUS.md">Polymur’s numerical bound with the required random key</a>, <a href="records/clhash/LEAN_CLHASH_STATUS.md">the mathematical CLHASH algorithm</a>, <a href="records/classic/LEAN_CLASSIC_STATUS.md">Poly1305 and single-message GHASH</a>, and <a href="records/highway-bridge/LEAN_HIGHWAY_STATUS.md">the counted HighwayHash collision event</a>. Supporting results cover <a href="records/LEAN_STATUS.md">polynomial hashing and other basic constructions</a>, <a href="records/brw-recurrence8/STATUS.md">BRW and the eight-lane recurrence</a>, and <a href="records/multiply-shift/LEAN_MULTISHIFT_STATUS.md">multiply-shift</a>.</p><p class="l-body">Some proof-checking work remains incomplete: <a href="records/nh-halftime/LEAN_NH_HALFTIME_STATUS.md">connecting the HalftimeHash executable to the mathematical argument</a>, <a href="records/umash/LEAN_UMASH_STATUS.md">formalizing UMASH’s corrected probability bound</a>, and adapting the original ChainHash proof to v2. The repaired HalftimeHash24 has a checked construction, but no complete C++ correctness proof. Throughout the post, a checked mathematical proof is distinct from verification of source code, compiler behavior or a deterministic key generator.</p></section>
<section aria-labelledby="prior-discussion" class="post-section l-page"><h2 class="l-body" id="prior-discussion">Prior discussion</h2><p class="l-body">Foldhash is Orson Peters’ hash: alongside his <a href="https://orlp.net/blog/breaking-hash-functions/">2024 post on breaking hash functions</a>, he ships both the non-universal foldhash default and <a href="https://github.com/orlp/polymur-hash">PolymurHash</a>, a universal alternative.</p><p class="l-body">I build on earlier mechanisms: CityHash and FarmHash build on <a href="https://orlp.net/blog/breaking-hash-functions/">Peters 2024</a> and <a href="https://events.ccc.de/congress/2012/Fahrplan/events/5152.en.html">Aumasson–Bernstein–Bosslet’s 29C3 2012 work</a>; MurmurHash3 builds on the 2012 top-bit construction. Peters’ <a href="https://github.com/ogxd/gxhash/issues/83">gxhash #83</a> and <a href="https://github.com/eternal-io/museair/issues/3">MuseAir #3</a> supply two more mechanisms. HighwayHash extends the designers’ cancellation analysis. I retain those credits in the NEW, REPRODUCTION and EXTENSION labels.</p><p class="l-body">The distinction between statistical quality, secret-seed guarantees and adaptive security predates this post. <a href="https://blog.cessen.com/post/2024_07_10_hash_design_and_goodharts_law">Nathan Vegdahl’s 2024 discussion</a>, explicitly about non-adversarial large-output hashes, argues that confidence needs “analysis and justification of their design.” <a href="https://orlp.net/blog/breaking-hash-functions/">Orson Peters’ 2024 conclusion</a> is on the same side: “With universal hashing it’s possible to construct hash functions for which such an attack is provably impossible”. He immediately limits that claim to non-interactive attackers. In the <a href="https://lobste.rs/s/zltznl/breaking_cityhash64_murmurhash2_3">discussion</a>, UMASH’s author Paul Khuong writes: “This speed VS guarantee on seed-independent collision rates is a false choice.”</p><p class="l-body">SMHasher’s author Austin Appleby describes its role this way. In <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=42169830">November 2024</a>: “if it passes SMHasher it should be fine for general use.” In his <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=42186477">follow-up</a>, the tests are “designed to break hash functions by generating low-entropy key sets that try to trigger weak bit-mixing paths”. I use those finite tests as evidence while asking for a bound over every admissible fixed pair.</p><p class="l-body">The <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=37184076">2023 nullc/Peters exchange</a> addresses truncation directly. Maxwell warns that an AU proof “won't apply to subsets of bits”; Peters replies: “PolymurHash has no such guarantee, the claims are only over the full hash.” <a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/1503.03465#page=2">Lemire–Kaser §2.1</a> supplies the mathematical distinction between AU and differential bounds. Lemire also <a href="https://lemire.me/blog/2015/10/26/crazily-fast-hashing-with-carry-less-multiplications/#comments">conceded the short-string tradeoff in 2015</a>: “For short strings, performance is likely driven by latency”.</p><p class="l-body">Keyed-security wording has been disputed before. <a href="https://github.com/google/highwayhash/issues/28">Rurban’s HighwayHash issue #28</a> raised the role of bucket reduction and collision resolution in 2016; that discussion is prior context, not evidence for this post’s new state collision. My earlier public <a href="https://github.com/Cyan4973/xxHash/issues/1127">xxHash #1127</a> is the report behind the XXH3 reproduction credit. The current <a href="https://github.com/orlp/foldhash#hashdos-resistance">foldhash README</a> takes an explicitly limited position: “foldhash does not claim to provide HashDoS resistance against interactive attackers”. It offers mitigation of simple attacks, not an AU theorem. <a href="https://github.com/rust-lang/hashbrown/blob/main/README.md">Hashbrown’s README</a> names foldhash as its default and warns that it provides less HashDoS resistance than SipHash. That is hashbrown’s choice; Rust’s standard HashMap separately documents SipHash.</p><p class="l-body">The stronger objection to linear universal hashing is already in <a href="https://www.aumasson.jp/siphash/siphash.pdf#page=14">the SipHash paper §7</a>: visible bucket relations, including timing feedback, can expose linear equations and permit more collisions. The <a href="https://www.aumasson.jp/siphash/siphashdos_29c3_slides.pdf">29C3 slides</a> propose SipHash as the remedy as well as demonstrating attacks. <a href="https://www.iacr.org/archive/crypto2008/51570145/51570145.pdf">Handschuh–Preneel (2008)</a> study key recovery against universal-hash-based MACs; that adaptive setting is stronger than this post’s fixed-pair experiment. <a href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2014/722">Dobraunig, Mendel and Schläffer (2014)</a> analyze SipHash’s differential behavior and reduced variants. I would state the parameters and model explicitly; SipHash has been cryptanalyzed.</p></section><div class="appendices l-page" data-toc-group="Appendix">
<section aria-labelledby="appendix-a" class="post-section l-page">
<h2 class="l-body" id="appendix-a">Appendix A. The hard cases, hash by hash</h2>
<p class="l-body">Each entry below starts with the practical finding, then gives the exact code version, message bytes and derivation. The pattern labels link to shared explanations of the arithmetic. “Every seed” means changing the seed cannot separate that pair; a sampled rate describes only the tested random-key experiment.</p>
<section class="post-section l-page recurring-patterns" aria-labelledby="recurring-patterns">
<h3 class="l-body" id="recurring-patterns">Recurring patterns</h3>
<p class="l-body">Most of these pairs exploit the same few identities. A secret added after information is lost cannot recover it; a second lane or a wider output cannot help if it repeats the same computation. The groups below describe the witnesses in these entries, not every path of each hash. A row can belong to more than one group. The generator examples are marked separately from the selected scoring pairs; “ungrouped” means the entry does not establish one of these mechanisms, not that the hash is safe.</p>

<h4 class="l-body" id="pattern-p1">P1. The folded multiply forgets complements</h4>
<p class="l-body">Let <span class="math" data-tex="B=2^{64}">B = 2<sup>64</sup></span> and <span class="math" data-tex="F(a,b)=\operatorname{lo}_{64}(ab)\oplus\operatorname{hi}_{64}(ab)">F(a,b) = lo₆₄(ab) ⊕ hi₆₄(ab)</span>. Complementing a message word also complements its XOR-masked operand. The exact integer identity is:<span class="math pattern-equation" data-tex="(\mathord{\sim}a)(\mathord{\sim}b)=ab+(2^{64}-1)(2^{64}-1-a-b)">(~a)(~b) = ab + (2<sup>64</sup>−1)(2<sup>64</sup>−1−a−b)</span>If the products have halves (lo, hi) and (lo′, hi′), the folds agree exactly when <span class="math" data-tex="\mathrm{lo}\oplus\mathrm{lo}'=\mathrm{hi}\oplus\mathrm{hi}'">lo ⊕ lo′ = hi ⊕ hi′</span>: the carry and borrow patterns can erase the change. The entries report roughly 2<sup>−27</sup> for several pairs, not a common theorem or rate for all seed mappings; XXH3-64’s selected pair has its own measurement. At eight bytes, foldhash reads the same word into both operands, so one complemented word suffices. XXH3-128 also preserves the raw sum by choosing w₁ = ~w₀. For the documented XXH3-64 32/128-byte pair, common tail folds change the output but not the colliding-seed set; quality’s extra fold likewise preserves every fast collision.</p>
<p class="l-body pattern-rows"><b>Rows:</b> <a href="#appendix-wyhash">wyhash</a>, <a href="#appendix-rapidhash-v1">rapidhash v1</a>, <a href="#appendix-rapidhash-v3">rapidhash v3</a>, <a href="#appendix-foldhash-fast">foldhash-fast</a>, <a href="#appendix-foldhash-quality">foldhash-quality</a>, <a href="#appendix-xxh3-64">XXH3-64</a>, <a href="#appendix-xxh3-128">XXH3-128</a>.</p>
<p class="l-body pattern-proof"><b>What a proof needs:</b> an XOR-universality bound for the keyed fold under the actual joint distribution of its masks, including the zero output difference used here.</p>

<h4 class="l-body" id="pattern-p2">P2. Public arithmetic leaves the seed out of the difference</h4>
<p class="l-body">For a seed-last wrapper <span class="math" data-tex="H_s(m)=G_s(C(m))">Hₛ(m) = Gₛ(C(m))</span>, the identity <span class="math" data-tex="C(m)=C(m')\implies H_s(m)=H_s(m')">C(m) = C(m′) ⇒ Hₛ(m) = Hₛ(m′)</span> holds for every seed, whether or not Gₛ is invertible. CityHash, FarmHash, gxhash and pengyhash lose the distinction in public compression. MUM and mir instead XOR an already-colliding public product term into their seeded state; their fold adds the product halves. MuseAir reaches <span class="math" data-tex="\mathrm{head}\oplus P(\mathrm{tail})\oplus K_s">head ⊕ P(tail) ⊕ Kₛ</span>, so a head change cancels any chosen public tail change at the same length. In mx3, h ← (h + g(w))C leaves a common seed coefficient after the same number of steps, and g is publicly invertible. Fasthash similarly lets an inverse word mix cancel the length term. MurmurHash3’s public word bijections let the attacker place, then cancel, the top-bit difference described in P7; the seed affects the state but does not hide that differential.</p>
<p class="l-body pattern-rows"><b>Rows:</b> <a href="#appendix-cityhash64">CityHash64</a>, <a href="#appendix-farmhash64">FarmHash64</a>, <a href="#appendix-gxhash">gxhash</a>, <a href="#appendix-pengyhash">pengyhash</a>, <a href="#appendix-mum">MUM v3</a>, <a href="#appendix-mir">mir</a>, <a href="#appendix-museair">MuseAir</a>, <a href="#appendix-mx3">mx3</a>, <a href="#appendix-fasthash">fasthash-32/64</a>, <a href="#appendix-murmurhash3">MurmurHash3</a>.</p>
<p class="l-body pattern-proof"><b>What a proof needs:</b> a collision bound for the message compression itself, with keys participating in the arithmetic that distinguishes messages; keying only the surrounding state or finalizer supplies no such bound.</p>

<h4 class="l-body" id="pattern-p3">P3. Zero absorbs the other operand</h4>
<p class="l-body">The identity is simply <span class="math" data-tex="0\cdot x=0\cdot x'=0">0 · x = 0 · x′ = 0</span>. In a5hash-128, a public operand can be set to zero for every seed. In a5hash-64, the first message word matches one value of the expanded state, zeroing an operand on its exactly counted seed fibre of density 118 × 2<sup>−45</sup>. Half a word suffices in HighwayHash’s lo₃₂(v₁)·hi₃₂(v₀) product: fixing hi₃₂(key[0]) makes hi₃₂(v₀) zero. That class has density 2<sup>−32</sup>, but a further conditional event of probability 56165/2<sup>40</sup> is required to merge the full state. HalftimeHash24’s equal-length Encode3 witness does collide throughout its high₃₂(core_key[6]) = 0 class, also of density 2<sup>−32</sup>; the encoding lets only one symbol change. These are different events, not a shared collision rate.</p>
<p class="l-body pattern-rows"><b>Rows:</b> <a href="#appendix-a5hash-128">a5hash-128</a>, <a href="#appendix-a5hash-64">a5hash-64</a>, <a href="#appendix-highwayhash">HighwayHash</a>, <a href="#appendix-halftime24">HalftimeHash24 (equal-length witness)</a>.</p>
<p class="l-body pattern-proof"><b>What a proof needs:</b> a bound on the actual key fibres that zero an operand, together with the conditional probability that the remaining state differences cancel.</p>

<h4 class="l-body" id="pattern-p4">P4. Related lanes repeat the same product</h4>
<p class="l-body">In komihash, a second lane inherits a public XOR offset: s₂ = s₁ ⊕ c. Choosing its message word as w₂ = w₁ ⊕ c gives <span class="math" data-tex="w_2\oplus s_2=(w_1\oplus c)\oplus(s_1\oplus c)=w_1\oplus s_1">w₂ ⊕ s₂ = (w₁ ⊕ c) ⊕ (s₁ ⊕ c) = w₁ ⊕ s₁</span>. Compensating the other operand’s public offset makes both lane products identical within each message. Their low halves cancel in the lane XOR; the chosen bit flips leave a common high-product change of at most one, which often vanishes or cancels through the additions. This explains the measured 0.9106 rate at lengths 64 through 127 bytes, where the fold follows immediately. The entry does not extend that result past another bulk round.</p>
<p class="l-body pattern-rows"><b>Row:</b> <a href="#appendix-komihash">komihash</a>.</p>
<p class="l-body pattern-proof"><b>What a proof needs:</b> independent lane keys, or a joint differential bound for their actual dependence, so an attacker cannot equate lane operands merely by cancelling public offsets.</p>

<h4 class="l-body" id="pattern-p5">P5. Length encoding can be cancelled</h4>
<p class="l-body">A length field only helps if it distinguishes the encoded inputs. On gxhash’s short path, <span class="math" data-tex="C_n(m)_i=(\operatorname{pad}(m)_i+n)\bmod256">Cₙ(m)ᵢ = (pad(m)ᵢ + n) mod 256</span>; fifteen zero bytes and sixteen ff bytes both become sixteen 0f bytes. HalftimeHash’s raw advanced core omits length, so empty and one zero byte coincide. Fasthash includes length, but one message step lets the attacker solve <span class="math" data-tex="\operatorname{mix}(w)=7m\oplus8m">mix(w) = 7m ⊕ 8m</span>, cancelling the difference between seven and eight bytes before the common multiply. For mx3’s one-byte/eight-byte pair, the corresponding equation is <span class="math" data-tex="g(w)=g(0)+C(g(2)-g(9))">g(w) = g(0) + C(g(2) − g(9))</span>. Both paths have the same seed coefficient. These are encoding or length-term aliases; t1ha2’s cross-length pair still requires the carry event in P7.</p>
<p class="l-body pattern-rows"><b>Rows:</b> <a href="#appendix-gxhash">gxhash</a>, <a href="#appendix-halftime24">HalftimeHash24 (raw core)</a>, <a href="#appendix-fasthash">fasthash-32/64</a>, <a href="#appendix-mx3">mx3</a>.</p>
<p class="l-body pattern-proof"><b>What a proof needs:</b> injective length framing before compression, or a keyed cross-length collision bound that accounts for how message words can cancel the length term.</p>

<h4 class="l-body" id="pattern-p6">P6. Equal-state blocks and public inverses generate families</h4>
<p class="l-body">A collision recipe can give more than a pair. MurmurHash3’s supporting 32-byte construction has <span class="math" data-tex="T_A(s)=T_B(s)">T_A(s) = T_B(s)</span> for the incoming state s: it resets the <em>difference</em> to zero, not the state to a fixed constant. Choosing A or B at each of n positions therefore gives 2<sup>n</sup> equal-length colliding messages. Separately, the CityHash README’s pair B solves a public word permutation for a chosen compressed value; three free words give 2<sup>192</sup> colliding 32-byte inputs. MuseAir’s invertible head encoding gives <span class="math" data-tex="\mathrm{head}'=\mathrm{head}\oplus P(T)\oplus P(T')">head′ = head ⊕ P(T) ⊕ P(T′)</span>, one partner for every tail of the chosen length. These are the documented generators; the selected shorter CityHash and MurmurHash3 pairs keep their original scores.</p>
<p class="l-body pattern-rows"><b>Rows:</b> <a href="#appendix-murmurhash3">MurmurHash3 (supporting two-block pair)</a> · <a href="verify/murmurhash3-128/README.md">generator record</a>; <a href="#appendix-cityhash64">CityHash64 (supporting pair B)</a> · <a href="verify/cityhash-64/README.md">inverse record</a>; <a href="#appendix-museair">MuseAir</a> · <a href="verify/museair/README.md">head–tail recipe</a>.</p>
<p class="l-body pattern-proof"><b>What a proof needs:</b> an injective encoding or a keyed collision bound for the joint message-to-state map; invertibility in one public input word makes these compensating words easy to solve and does not make the whole map injective.</p>

<h4 class="l-body" id="pattern-p7">P7. Top bits and carries carry a difference through</h4>
<p class="l-body">At word width w, let t = 2<sup>w−1</sup>. Modulo 2<sup>w</sup>, <span class="math" data-tex="x\oplus t=x+t,\qquad (x\oplus t)c=(xc)\oplus t\quad(c\text{ odd})">x ⊕ t = x + t, and (x ⊕ t)c = (xc) ⊕ t for odd c</span>. Public rotations and XOR shifts can place a difference at that top bit; a later message word cancels it. MurmurHash3, fasthash’s supporting equal-length pair and nmhash32x use this deterministic identity. nmhash32 works in 16-bit multiplication lanes and additionally constrains an addition and a carry across bit 13. More generally, <span class="math" data-tex="x\oplus d=x+d-2(x\mathbin{\&amp;}d)">x ⊕ d = x + d − 2(x &amp; d)</span>, so a fixed XOR change can become opposite signed additive changes. SpookyHash’s late injection and tail cancel for the favourable sign; exact half-seed balance is unproved. t1ha2’s seeded-product carries instead give a class of density 2<sup>−26</sup> with measured conditional success about 2<sup>−4</sup>, not a zero-operand identity.</p>
<p class="l-body pattern-rows"><b>Rows:</b> <a href="#appendix-murmurhash3">MurmurHash3</a>, <a href="#appendix-fasthash">fasthash-32/64 (supporting equal-length pair)</a>, <a href="#appendix-nmhash32">nmhash32</a>, <a href="#appendix-nmhash32x">nmhash32x</a>, <a href="#appendix-spookyhash">SpookyHash V2</a>, <a href="#appendix-t1ha2">t1ha2</a>.</p>
<p class="l-body pattern-proof"><b>What a proof needs:</b> a differential bound through the actual word widths and joint carry conditions, rather than an assumption that rotations, odd multipliers or individual state bits behave independently.</p>
<p class="l-body">I leave <a href="#appendix-ahash">aHash’s AES path</a> ungrouped: its entry needs an inverse-AES differential and simultaneous shuffled-addition cancellation under independent keys, which is not established by the identities above. The proof and comparison entries also receive no collision-pattern assignment. Neither a proof gap nor a shared multiplication instruction is enough to assign a row.</p>
</section>

