<p class="l-body"><b>Rust transfer.</b> The evidence is a validated C reproduction of the specified aHash 0.8.12 AES path under independent internal keys. It models hashing <code>&amp;[u8]</code>: <code>write_usize(len)</code>, then <code>write(bytes)</code>, including the overlapping reads and both length effects. <a href="https://github.com/tkaitchuck/aHash/blob/v0.8.12/src/random_state.rs#L303"><code>RandomState::with_seeds(a,b,c,d)</code></a> XORs each argument with the corresponding public PI2 word. Thus independent uniform arguments map bijectively to independent internal words; passing the same seed four times is a different distribution. <a href="https://github.com/tkaitchuck/aHash/blob/v0.8.12/src/aes_hash.rs#L72"><code>from_random_state</code></a> initializes the two accumulators from those word pairs and their XOR. No native Rust run is supplied here. Transfer to a deployed key type or write sequence is conditional on that mapping and the AES backend; a direct <code>Hasher::write</code> call is not silently interchangeable. The crate selects AES with the target AES feature on x86, while its ARM AES path additionally requires <code>nightly-arm-aes</code>.</p></section>
