<pre aria-label="Numbered pseudo-code" class="l-middle pseudocode" tabindex="0"><code class="nohighlight"><span class="code-line" id="code-t1ha-1"><span aria-hidden="true" class="line-number">01</span>// Input: M[len], 9 &lt;= len &lt;= 16; secret seed (64 bits).</span>
<span class="code-line" id="code-t1ha-2"><span aria-hidden="true" class="line-number">02</span>const P0 = 0xec99bf0d8372caab, P1 = 0x82434fe90edcef39; // Public.</span>
<span class="code-line" id="code-t1ha-3"><span aria-hidden="true" class="line-number">03</span>const P2 = 0xd4f06db99d67be4b, P5 = 0xc060724a8424f345;</span>
<span class="code-line" id="code-t1ha-4"><span aria-hidden="true" class="line-number">04</span>const P6 = 0xcb5af53ae3aaac31;</span>
<span class="code-line" id="code-t1ha-5"><span aria-hidden="true" class="line-number">05</span>a = seed; b = len;                  // Byte length enters the state.</span>
<span class="code-line" id="code-t1ha-6"><span aria-hidden="true" class="line-number">06</span>w0 = read64le(M, 0);</span>
<span class="code-line" id="code-t1ha-7"><span aria-hidden="true" class="line-number">07</span>w1 = read_le_zero_extended(M + 8, len - 8); // 8 or 3 bytes in this pair.</span>
<span class="code-line" id="code-t1ha-8"><span aria-hidden="true" class="line-number">08</span>(lo, hi) = mul128(b + w0, P2);       // First product is public.</span>
<span class="code-line" id="code-t1ha-9"><span aria-hidden="true" class="line-number">09</span>a ^= lo; b += hi;</span>
<span class="code-line" id="code-t1ha-10"><span aria-hidden="true" class="line-number">10</span>(lo, hi) = mul128(a + w1, P1);       // Exploit: carries in this seeded product.</span>
<span class="code-line" id="code-t1ha-11"><span aria-hidden="true" class="line-number">11</span>b ^= lo; a += hi;                   // mixup64(&amp;b, &amp;a, w1, P1).</span>
<span class="code-line" id="code-t1ha-12"><span aria-hidden="true" class="line-number">12</span>x = (a + rotl(b, 23)) * P0;         // Source rotr(b, 41).</span>
<span class="code-line" id="code-t1ha-13"><span aria-hidden="true" class="line-number">13</span>y = (rotl(a, 41) + b) * P6;         // Source rotr(a, 23).</span>
<span class="code-line" id="code-t1ha-14"><span aria-hidden="true" class="line-number">14</span>return fold(x ^ y, P5);</span></code></pre>
<p class="l-body source-note">Code: <a href="https://github.com/erthink/t1ha">t1ha</a>; <a href="https://gitlab.com/fwojcik/smhasher3/-/blob/main/hashes/t1ha.cpp">SMHasher3 hashes/t1ha.cpp</a>, t1ha2_atonce v2.1.4; tested at upstream commit <a href="https://gitlab.com/fwojcik/smhasher3/-/blob/7ad8939d/hashes/t1ha.cpp">7ad8939d</a>; local copy: <a href="sources/t1ha.cpp">sources/t1ha.cpp</a>. Excerpt: constants, mixup64, final64, T1HA2_TAIL and t1ha2, source lines 180–186, 748–760, 800–862 and 1144–1180.</p><p class="l-body">Both messages take the short path in <a href="#code-t1ha-5">lines 5–11</a>. Their first words and lengths determine public products (<a href="#code-t1ha-8">line 8</a>); the tail then meets a seed-dependent multiplication in <a href="#code-t1ha-10">line 10</a>. A carry-pattern search finds differences that cancel in both state words for a structured seed class. The class fixes 26 bits of a bijective image of the seed, so its density is exactly <span class="math" data-tex="2^{-26}">2<sup>−26</sup></span>. The measured conditional collision rate is about <span class="math" data-tex="2^{-4}">2<sup>−4</sup></span>, giving an estimated contribution of <span class="math" data-tex="2^{-30}">2<sup>−30</sup></span> to the unconditional collision probability and an estimated score cap of 31 bits.</p>
<p class="l-body" id="pair-t1ha"><b>t1ha2-64 — NEW.</b> The following pair uses different lengths:</p>
<pre class="l-page" tabindex="0"><code class="c">/* Hex encodes bytes in memory order, not hexadecimal integers. */
A = hex("406b68c281a55e00158ed10a0d96f8ff");
B = hex("d1d86241d24d9f990323e0");

/* Example seed: */
0x3c805cc67a687f30
/* Both 64-bit hashes: 0xf2a1196d24fddaab */</code></pre><p class="l-body"><b>Length and word difference.</b> 16 and 11 bytes; <span class="math" data-tex="L=2">L = 2</span>. Zero-based little-endian words are shown below; a final partial word is zero-extended only for displaying the difference, not for the hash input.</p><pre class="l-page" tabindex="0"><code class="nohighlight">w[0]: 0x005ea581c2686b40 -&gt; 0x999f4dd24162d8d1 (XOR 0x99c1e853830ab391)
w[1]: 0xfff8960d0ad18e15 -&gt; 0x0000000000e02303 (XOR 0xfff8960d0a31ad16)</code></pre><p class="l-body">The second model family independently derived that class and observed 262,144 collisions in <span class="math" data-tex="2^{22}">2<sup>22</sup></span> class samples. The exact sample ratio does not prove an exact population probability: carry signs and input wrap still matter. Nor does the 31-bit result apply to an equal-length-only model. The best equal-length claim in the original investigation was not independently rerun, and is omitted here.</p><p class="l-body">With class density <span class="math" data-tex="2^{-26}">2<sup>−26</sup></span> and the measured conditional rate about <span class="math" data-tex="2^{-4}">2<sup>−4</sup></span>, the estimated unconditional contribution is <span class="math" data-tex="2^{-30}">2<sup>−30</sup></span>; at <span class="math" data-tex="L=2">L = 2</span> the estimated score cap is <b>31 bits</b>.</p>
<p class="l-body"><b>Reproduction.</b> From the supplied <a href="verify/t1ha2-64/README.md">t1ha2-64/README.md</a>, run in <code>verify/t1ha2-64/</code>:</p>
<pre class="l-page" tabindex="0"><code class="nohighlight">cc -O2 -std=c11 -o t1ha2_64_verify t1ha2_64_verify.c -lm
./t1ha2_64_verify            # 2^24 seeds per experiment, rng seed 1 (about 1.3 s)</code></pre>
<p class="l-body"><b>New.</b> This carry-pattern construction is new in the supplied records.</p>
<p class="l-body" data-score-id="t1ha"><b>Selected score (t1ha2_atonce-64 v2.1.4): ≈ 31* bits.</b> 16/11 bytes; <span class="math" data-tex="L=2">L = 2</span>. <span class="math" data-tex="2^{-26}\cdot(262144/2^{22})\approx2^{-30}">2^-26 × (262144/2^22) ≈ 2^-30</span>, estimated class contribution. See <a href="#metric">The collision score</a>.</p><!-- BEGIN HASH DEMO appendix-t1ha2 -->
