<p class="l-body">The selected 32-byte witness gives an estimated cap of 28.5 bits from <span class="math" data-tex="11/2^{30}">11/2<sup>30</sup></span> events. Its transformed exact Poisson 95% interval is [27.7, 29.5] bits. This is uncertainty in the witness-derived cap, not a confidence interval for the unknown worst pair.</p></section>
<section aria-labelledby="appendix-xxh3-64" class="post-section l-page hash-case"><h3 class="l-body" id="appendix-xxh3-64" data-toc-label="XXH3-64 0.8.3">XXH3-64 0.8.3 <span class="pattern-tags"><span class="pattern-label">pattern:</span> <a href="#pattern-p1" title="fold complements">P1</a></span></h3>
<p class="l-body reader-summary">The selected message changes sometimes preserve a multiplication result and hence the full hash. Several pairs appear in this entry; their different lengths and measurements must be kept separate.</p><p class="l-body">The selected REPRODUCTION uses XXH3_64bits_withSeed 0.8.3 with its default public secret and the 32-byte, zero-tail version of base 1143. It scores L=4; the historical 128-byte extension scores L=16 for the same colliding-seed event.</p><pre class="l-body"><code>M  = hex("8912a3da9fc464368202a1be238b7d1100000000000000000000000000000000");
M' = hex("76ed5c25603b9bc97dfd5e41dc7482ee00000000000000000000000000000000");
// F = low64(a*b) XOR high64(a*b), with seeded a,b.
// At 32 B: return avalanche(32*PRIME64_1 + F + unchanged_fold).
// At 128 B: seven unchanged folds replace the one unchanged fold.
// The final avalanche is a permutation.</code></pre><p class="l-body">Fresh streams found 483 and 535 collisions per 2<sup>30</sup> keys at 32 bytes, and exactly the same events at 128 bytes. The separate central exact-Poisson 95% ε intervals are [4.1060167657147987e−7, 4.917927147669023e−7] and [4.569258159359195e−7, 5.423240808782643e−7]. Pooling only these two fresh streams gives 1018/2<sup>31</sup> = 0.000000474043190479278564453125, interval [4.453666711465765e−7, 5.04081714020458e−7], and a 32-byte score cap estimate 23.008478153924305 with interval [22.919839043866432, 23.098503065606845]. The 128-byte score is exactly two bits higher, so the shorter confirmed pair is scored.</p><p class="l-body">At 16 bytes, the different path produced 0/2<sup>32</sup> collisions across all four tested streams: central 95% ε interval [0, 8.588841776628828e−10], transformed score interval [31.11681735485275, +∞]. Zero events supply no finite empirical collision cap, so 16 bytes is not scored. Historical replays at 32 bytes found 504 and 526 per 2<sup>30</sup>; they reproduce existing observations and are not pooled with their originals, and the correlated 32/128-byte tests are not independent samples.</p><p class="l-body">The older 9/12/11/11 cross-hash comparison belongs to the separate 32-byte pair A, not base 1143. The <a href="records/xxh3_32B_RESULT.md">result report</a> gives both lengths, every exact interval and the historical provenance; <a href="records/xxh3_32B_result.json">JSON</a> preserves symbolic expressions. The <a href="verify/xxh3-64/xxh3_64_32B_check.c">new C program</a> verifies 16/32/128-byte behavior and known witnesses; <a href="verify/xxh3-64/CHECK_32B.md">reproduction instructions</a> distinguish smoke counts from large samples. This fixed-pair rate establishes no practical flooding attack or theorem for independently randomized custom-secret APIs.</p><p class="l-body">In the <a href="https://github.com/Cyan4973/xxHash/issues/1127#issuecomment-5720905442">17 September maintainer reply</a>, Yann Collet thanked the reporter, summarized the carefully crafted-pair result and its approximately one-in-100-million rate, reiterated XXH3’s noncryptographic guarantee scope, and said the mixing-function comment will be updated. That reply concerns the earlier report; it is not a validation of the fresh base-1143 rate.</p></section>
<section aria-labelledby="appendix-xxh3-128" class="post-section l-page hash-case">
<h3 class="l-body" id="appendix-xxh3-128" data-toc-label="XXH3-128 0.8.3">XXH3-128 0.8.3 <span class="pattern-tags"><span class="pattern-label">pattern:</span> <a href="#pattern-p1" title="fold complements">P1</a></span></h3>
<p class="l-body reader-summary">Producing a full 128-bit collision requires preserving both a product-related value and a sum. The pair is chosen to do both, though the measured probability rests on a small collision count.</p>
<figure class="mechanism-figure l-body" id="fig-xxh3-128">
